Kokujin the chaotic monk started out a monk with a blessed scroll of 
charging and a spellbook of healing. His hit points went up about 6-8 
hitpoints each level which pleased me. He dropped a couple of rings down 
a sink finding that they were gain constitution, protection and conflict. 
A level later he discovered a ring of slow digestion which made my quest 
less of an ordeal. At the first shop he encountered there were a pair of 
speed boots. Sadly they were turned to dust after reading an unidentified 
scroll of destroy armor. Kokujin's robe becomes cursed and loses an 
enchantment from a cursed scroll of enchant armor. At least that's 
identified and out of the way.

Kokujin's descent through the mines was interrupted by a polymorph trap 
which turned me into a salamander. My first thought, "Oh great. A tiny 
thing that could get its ass kicked by a newt" and then saw gnomes get 
crushed and roasted in his grasp. The descent quickened with a 
smouldering wake in his path.

At Mine's End he kills a cockatrice who immediately gets wielded for 
pragmatism's sake. By this time Kokujin's past level nine and finding 
traps which certainly helps in avoiding pits while wielding a rubber 
chicken. He found the luckstone and then its cousin the loadstone. At 
least he never reached being stressed but being burdened isn't any fun. 
Back in Minetown he starts selling the items that were found along the 
way and get waylaid by a mumak in a general store. These beasties have 
always been worse for me than a combination of a floating eye and a newt, 
much moreso than a mindflayer. Two hits and the mumak goes down much to 
my relief. Five fountains later and the loadstone's uncursed and he can 
go about his merry way.

On level eight of the dungeons Kokujin find a spellbook of drain life. My 
only thought was "I'm being set up" and decide to meet fate head-on by 
being stupid rather than facing the wrath of the RNG. Kokujin throws 
himself into a room filled with Mordor orcs and proceeds to kick their 
collective asses. Among the corpses there's a scroll of enchant armor 
which makes him strip down to only his cursed robe and it glows silver 
for a while. I check his inventory and the robe has become blessed! Is it 
normal for blessed scrolls of enchant armor to bless items?

Kokujin stumbled upon his second polymorph trap but at least he was 
turned into something reasonable rather than some enormous monster. 
Kokujin the Tengu starts looking for a closet to lock himself in and wait 
out the polymorph. Around then I discover that tengus can only bite 
rather than claw, scratch, wield a weapon which makes me worry about 
Kokojin's vegan conduct. Rather than the usual feeding spree on pudding 
corpses (non-black, of course) to get early intrinsics I was letting 
those corpses rot while proceeding with my descent.

There's a mad dash through the big room because there were two gargoyles 
descending upon me. Once Kokujin reaches the level below he receives a 
faint telepathic message from the Grand Master. That level had a 
leprechaun hall which made him a very rich monk and a desire to donate to 
the temple since a monk must shun gross things such as material wealth. 
Next level is the rogue level and after that there's a room with an 
archon.

Damn.

Being chaotic he wants to avoid these like anyone would avoid a lich. He 
locks him in the room and explores the level until he came across a 
vampire.
Easy meat.
The vampire quickly drains me of two levels and only recover one after 
killing his leech ass.

I have no idea how this happened but somehow during the fray Kokujin lost 
his gloves. The next target was a chickatrice who got punched and 
immediately created a delightful statue of Kokujin.

After the obligatory DYWYPI, I learned that I did keep my vegan conduct!

Being a monster that bites, rather than eats, does not harm vegan or 
vegetarian conduct. I'm sure if you're a vampire and I know if you become 
a mindflayer that'll completely mess with one's eating conduct.

